\description{
The Racing Diffusion Model (RDM) treats a choice as a race between two
diffusion processes, one per response option, each accumulating evidence at
its own rate until it reaches a common threshold. The winner determines both
the observed reaction time and the choice. The observed RT is that decision
time shifted by a non-decision time \code{ndt}, and a fixed proportion \code{poutlier}
of responses is generated by an outlier process instead of by the race.

Functions:
\itemize{
\item \code{rcogmod_rdm()}: Simulates random draws from the RDM.
\item \code{dcogmod_rdm()}: Computes the density (likelihood).
\item \code{pcogmod_rdm()}: Computes the CDF of the reaction time.
\item \code{cogmod_rdm()}: Creates a \code{brms::custom_family()} for use in \code{brms} models.
\item \code{cogmod_rdm_stanvars()}: Generates the \code{stanvars} to pass to \code{brm()}.
\item \code{p_outlier()}: Per-trial posterior probability of being an outlier.
}
}
\details{
\code{pcogmod_rdm()} describes the RT of the trial as a whole - whichever
accumulator wins, and whether or not the trial came from the outlier
component - since \code{P(min(T0, T1) > q) = S0(q) * S1(q)}. There is no comparably
simple closed form for the per-response defective CDF, so \code{pcogmod_rdm()}
takes no \code{response} argument.
}
\section{Parameterization}{
Each accumulator is a diffusion with drift rate \code{v} and unit diffusion
coefficient, starting from a point \code{z ~ Uniform(0, bias)} drawn afresh on
every trial and finishing when it reaches the threshold \code{b = boundary + bias}.
The distance it has to cover is therefore \code{b - z = boundary + bias - z},
which is what makes \code{boundary} the threshold \emph{offset}: the distance from the
highest possible starting point to the threshold. Its first passage time is
Wald (inverse Gaussian) with the start point integrated out. The observed
reaction time is \code{ndt + min(T_0, T_1)} and the observed choice is whichever
accumulator got there first.

This is the \code{B} parameterization of \code{DMC} and \code{EMC2}, where \code{b = B + A} with
\code{A} the start-point range (\code{bias} here). It is used for a reason rather than for taste: the
threshold has to sit above the highest possible starting point, and writing
the \emph{offset} makes \code{b > A} hold automatically for any positive value. The
alternative - estimating the absolute threshold, as \code{rtdists} does - needs an
order constraint between two estimated parameters, which has to hold in every
cell of the design once either of them carries a predictor. The cost is that
\code{boundary} alone is not the quantity to read off a fitted model; \code{boundary + bias} is.

A drift rate of \strong{exactly zero is allowed}, and is not the same as an
accumulator that never responds: driftless Brownian motion still reaches any
positive level with probability one, so a zero-drift accumulator is slow but
still finishes, and can still win the race.

\code{ndt} is expressed \strong{directly, in seconds} (through a log link in the \code{brms}
family). Nothing about it is taken from the data: it is not bounded by the
fastest observed response, so a non-decision time that varies by condition or
by participant can exceed the sample minimum wherever the data support it.

This replaces the earlier \code{tau} / \code{minrt} pair, in which \code{ndt = tau * minrt}
with \code{minrt} set to the fastest observed RT. That capped the non-decision
time at an order statistic of the sample, so any condition or participant
whose true \code{ndt} exceeded the fastest observed response was inexpressible,
and the misfit surfaced as spurious effects on the race parameters.
}

\section{The outlier component}{
A shifted distribution assigns exactly zero density to any response faster
than \code{ndt}, which puts a hard boundary in the likelihood at the fastest
observed RT. Mixing in a component with support over the whole positive line
removes it: every response keeps positive density whatever \code{ndt} is, so the
boundary becomes a finite cost rather than a wall and the log-density stays
smooth and differentiable. That is what makes the direct parameterization of
\code{ndt} workable without taking a bound from the data.

Because this model produces a \strong{choice as well as a time}, the contaminant
has to produce both. It is a guess: the choice is uniform over the two
options, and the RT is a \strong{half Normal} with scale \code{0.2} seconds.

\deqn{f(t, k) = p \frac{1}{K} g(t) + (1 - p) f_k(t - ndt)}

The \code{1 / K} is what keeps the total summing to one over the response options;
without it it would come to \code{1 + poutlier}. The half-t is used for the timing
because it is \strong{flat at the origin} (zero derivative), so the very fastest
responses - the ones least plausibly decisions - are not starved of density,
and because its tails are heavy enough to cover the whole plausible RT range.

\code{poutlier} is a \emph{rate}, not a classification: the model never labels
individual trials, it estimates what share of them came from elsewhere. Use
\code{\link[=p_outlier]{p_outlier()}} for per-trial posterior probabilities.
}

\section{Reaction times must be in seconds}{
The outlier component's scale is a constant in seconds, and so are the
priors \code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} supplies. There is no argument for changing the
unit: the \code{minrt} argument that used to rescale the component was removed in
0.2.1. Millisecond data fails silently rather than loudly - the outlier
component contributes nothing and the min-RT boundary comes back. See the
corresponding section of \code{\link[=cogmod_lognormal]{cogmod_lognormal()}} for the full account, which
applies unchanged here.
}

\section{Fitting}{
\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{f <- brms::bf(RT | dec(Error) ~ Condition, driftone ~ Condition,
              sigmabias ~ 1, boundary ~ 1, ndt ~ 1, poutlier ~ 1,
              family = cogmod_rdm())
brms::brm(f, data = df,
          prior    = cogmod_priors(f, df),
          init     = cogmod_inits(f, df),
          stanvars = cogmod_stanvars(f))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The \code{brms} family names the drift of the first accumulator \code{mu} (as \code{brms}
requires) and that of the second \code{driftone}, and calls the start-point range
\code{sigmabias} to match \code{\link[=cogmod_lba2]{cogmod_lba2()}}, where it denotes the same quantity. Note
that this is \emph{not} the same thing as \code{bias} in \code{\link[=cogmod_ddm]{cogmod_ddm()}}, which is a
relative starting point in \verb{[0, 1]}. Both drifts use a \code{softplus} link with a
lower bound of zero, following \code{\link[=cogmod_invgaussian]{cogmod_invgaussian()}}: a Wald drift must be
non-negative for the accumulator to be a proper first passage time.

Use \code{\link[=cogmod_inits]{cogmod_inits()}} rather than \code{init = 0}. \code{brms} initialises on the
unconstrained scale, so \code{init = 0} puts \code{ndt} at \code{exp(0) = 1} second - above
nearly every sub-second RT, which leaves every response attributed to the
outlier component and the race parameters with no gradient at all.

\code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} is not a convenience here either. Beyond \code{ndt} and
\code{poutlier}, \code{sigmabias} and \code{boundary} are only weakly identified from each
other, because they enter the threshold only through the sum
\code{b = boundary + sigmabias} and trade off almost freely: on simulated data with
4000 trials the profile log-likelihood varies by only about 3 units as
\code{sigmabias} ranges from 0 to half the threshold, while \code{boundary} slides to
compensate. With flat priors the sampler tends to wander down the
\code{sigmabias -> 0} ridge (the plain Wald race) and produce divergent
transitions, and a \code{softplus} link reaches zero only at minus infinity - a
flat prior over an unbounded flat region, which is an improper posterior.
\code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} fences both off, exactly as it does for \code{\link[=cogmod_lba1]{cogmod_lba1()}},
which shares this parameterisation.

The sum \code{boundary + sigmabias} is well identified either way, so it is the
more trustworthy quantity to interpret and to compare across conditions. The
same caveat applies to \code{\link[=cogmod_lba2]{cogmod_lba2()}}.
}

\section{Predictions exclude the outlier component}{
\code{posterior_predict()} describes the \strong{race alone} by default, as if
\code{poutlier} were zero, because the outlier component is a fixed regularizer
rather than a claim about how guesses are distributed. Use \code{\link[=with_outliers]{with_outliers()}}
for the fitted mixture - chiefly for \code{brms::pp_check()} - and
\code{\link[=without_outliers]{without_outliers()}} to go back. \code{log_lik()} is always the full mixture.

\code{posterior_epred()} is not provided: for a race model the expectation needs
numerical integration per draw and per observation, and users are better off
summarising \code{posterior_predict()} draws.
}

